<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<%- include(`${templatePath}/shared2026/head`, {
  headTitle: 'Reducing Checkout Friction After Renters Are Ready to Book — Vicky Wang',
  headDescription: 'A web checkout redesign that moved identity before checkout and the marketing survey after purchase, improving completion while revealing the next challenge: mobile verification.',
  headRobots: 'noindex, follow',
  headType: 'article',
  headImage: 'https://vickywang.me/thumbnails/cs_checkout.png',
  headStylesheet: '/worklog/bq_b2c_checkout/style.css'
}) %>

<body>

  <%- include(`${templatePath}/shared2026/header`, {
    headerVariant: 'detail',
    headerHome: '/worklog',
    headerAvatar: '../../web_images/photo_profile_avatar.png',
    headerLinks: [
      { href: '#problem', label: 'Problem', muted: true },
      { href: '#insight', label: 'Insight', muted: true },
      { href: '#design', label: 'Design', muted: true },
      { href: '#results', label: 'Results', muted: true }
    ]
  }) %>

  <main>
    <section class="cs-hero container cs-section--sm">
      <div class="cs-hero-meta">
        <span class="case-index">&#8470; 3 &middot; Q2 2026 &middot; BabyQuip</span>
        <span class="tag">DEMAND SIDE: CHECKOUT</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="cs-h2 cs-h2--display">Reducing Checkout Friction After Renters Are Ready to Book</h1>
      <p class="cs-hero-sub cs-hero-sub--wide">By checkout, renters have already decided. The old flow still asked them to create a password, answer a marketing survey, and second-guess their cart. I restructured checkout so it holds only what's needed to complete the order, and moved everything else to before or after it.</p>

      <div class="cs-divider-grid cs-divider-grid--hero checkout-stats">
        <div>
          <div class="cs-stat-value--sm">76.3% &rarr; <span class="accent">79.7%</span></div>
          <div class="cs-stat-label">Mobile web checkout completion</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-stat-value--sm">88.6% &rarr; <span class="accent">91.2%</span></div>
          <div class="cs-stat-label">Desktop web checkout completion</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-stat-value--sm">84.2%</div>
          <div class="cs-stat-label">Native app, unchanged</div>
          <div class="cs-stat-note">85.1% before &middot; The comparison group</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cs-stat-note checkout-measurement">Start checkout &rarr; purchase, 4 weeks before vs. 4 weeks after launch (GA4). Not an A/B test; <a class="cs-inline-link" href="#results">see Results for how to read this</a>.</p>

      <div class="cs-divider-grid cs-divider-grid--tight cs-divider-grid--bleed">
        <div>
          <div class="cs-label">ROLE</div>
          <div class="cs-text">Product designer. I led the web checkout redesign from diagnosis to shipped flow.</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-label">SCOPE</div>
          <div class="cs-text">Desktop and mobile web. The native app was out of scope.</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-label">COLLABORATORS</div>
          <div class="cs-text">VP Product Design, product manager, CTO, engineering, QA, marketing VP, customer support.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="problem" class="cs-section container">
      <div class="cs-kicker">01 &middot; PROBLEM</div>
      <h2 class="cs-h2">Checkout was doing five jobs, and only one of them was checking out</h2>
      <p class="cs-lead">By checkout, renters have chosen their gear, dates, and provider. What's left is confirming logistics, paying, and knowing what happens next. The legacy flow wrapped that in other jobs:</p>

      <div class="cs-content-grid cs-content-grid--steps checkout-legacy u-mt-md">
        <figure>
          <div class="cs-media cs-ar-standard">
            <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_legacy_process01.png" alt="Legacy step 0: choosing guest checkout or log in" data-modal-gallery="checkout-legacy" loading="lazy">
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <div class="cs-label u-mt-sm">STEP 0</div>
            <p class="cs-text u-fs-sm"><mark>Guest checkout or log in</mark></p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="cs-media cs-ar-standard">
            <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_legacy_process02.png" alt="Legacy step 1: customer information and an order-summary edit link back to the cart" data-modal-gallery="checkout-legacy" loading="lazy">
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <div class="cs-label u-mt-sm">STEP 1</div>
            <p class="cs-text u-fs-sm">Customer information</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="cs-media cs-ar-standard">
            <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_legacy_process03.png" alt="Legacy step 2: delivery or pickup details and notes" data-modal-gallery="checkout-legacy" loading="lazy">
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <div class="cs-label u-mt-sm">STEP 2</div>
            <p class="cs-text u-fs-sm">Delivery or pickup details and notes</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="cs-media cs-ar-standard">
            <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_legacy_process04.png" alt="Legacy step 3: required marketing survey before payment" data-modal-gallery="checkout-legacy" loading="lazy">
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <div class="cs-label u-mt-sm">STEP 3</div>
            <p class="cs-text u-fs-sm"><mark>Marketing survey</mark> and additional notes</p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <div class="cs-media cs-ar-standard">
            <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_legacy_process05.png" alt="Legacy step 4: payment and account creation at the end of checkout" data-modal-gallery="checkout-legacy" loading="lazy">
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            <div class="cs-label u-mt-sm">STEP 4</div>
            <p class="cs-text u-fs-sm">Payment, tip, cancellation option, rental responsibility, and <mark>account creation</mark></p>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="cs-media-caption">Highlighted: jobs that could happen outside checkout. Select a screenshot to view the legacy flow.</p>

      <h3 class="cs-h3 u-mt-lg">What I saw in Hotjar recordings and GA4</h3>
      <%# Add the reviewed Hotjar session count here when it is available. %>
      <div class="cs-content-grid cs-content-grid--fixed u-mt-md">
        <div class="cs-rule-top cs-rule-top--strong">
          <h4 class="cs-subhead">Account creation came last, after all the effort</h4>
          <p class="cs-text">Renters typed their email early, filled out most of the form, and then had to create a password that met the rules and type it twice. Some left there. Guest checkout had a worse failure: if the email already had an account, account creation failed and the renter had to start over.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-rule-top cs-rule-top--strong">
          <h4 class="cs-subhead">A marketing survey sat between delivery details and payment</h4>
          <p class="cs-text">It was required, and it served marketing rather than the renter.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-rule-top cs-rule-top--strong">
          <h4 class="cs-subhead">Exits pulled renters back into deciding</h4>
          <p class="cs-text">An edit link in the order summary sent them back to the cart, trip details were easy to reopen, and a &ldquo;how it works&rdquo; link and promo banners competed for attention.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-rule-top cs-rule-top--strong">
          <h4 class="cs-subhead">Mobile made all of it heavier</h4>
          <p class="cs-text">Long scrolling and a heavy footer made the order hard to review. Before launch, 76% of mobile web checkouts completed, compared with 89% on desktop.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="insight" class="cs-section container">
      <div class="cs-kicker">02 &middot; INSIGHT</div>
      <h2 class="cs-h2">Checkout is where a decision is carried out, not made</h2>
      <p class="cs-lead">Every earlier stage of the rental journey is about deciding: what I need, whether it fits my child, whether I trust the provider, whether the dates work, what it costs. By checkout, the renter has decided. The interface's job changes from helping them choose to staying out of the way.</p>

      <div class="cs-journey-grid">
        <div class="cs-journey-item">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">1</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Need clarity</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;What do I need for this trip?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-journey-item">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">2</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Fit clarity</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;Is this the right item for my child and trip?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-journey-item">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">3</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Provider trust</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;Can I trust this provider?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-journey-item">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">4</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Availability &amp; logistics</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;Can this work for my dates and location?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-journey-item">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">5</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Price confidence</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;Do I understand what this will cost?&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cs-journey-item cs-journey-item--active">
          <div class="cs-journey-num">6</div>
          <div class="cs-journey-label">Commitment</div>
          <div class="cs-quote">&ldquo;I'm ready, don't interrupt me.&rdquo;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-pullquote">
        <p>That gave me one rule for every element in the old flow: <span class="accent">if it isn't needed to complete this order, it moves before checkout or after it.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="design" class="cs-section container">
      <div class="cs-kicker">03 &middot; DESIGN</div>
      <h2 class="cs-h2">Before, during, and after checkout</h2>

      <div class="checkout-zones u-mt-md" aria-label="Where each task belongs">
        <div>
          <div class="cs-label">BEFORE</div>
          <p class="cs-text">Resolve identity with an email code</p>
        </div>
        <div class="checkout-zone-active">
          <div class="cs-label">DURING</div>
          <p class="cs-text">Confirm logistics, review the order, and pay</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-label">AFTER</div>
          <p class="cs-text">Answer the survey and explore the app</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-solutions">
        <div>
          <div class="cs-solution-head">
            <div class="cs-label">BEFORE CHECKOUT</div>
            <h3><span class="cs-solution-num">1 &middot;</span> Resolve identity first</h3>
            <p class="checkout-ownership">Team decision. The PM proposed one-time-code login; I designed the flow.</p>
            <p class="cs-text">We removed guest checkout and replaced password creation with a one-time email code, entered before checkout begins. The trade was deliberate: one light step for everyone up front, instead of a late step that could fail after the renter had already done the work. It removed the existing-email conflict, the password rules, and the risk of losing a filled-out form at the very end.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-before-after">
            <figure>
              <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
                <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_solution01.png" alt="Sign up or log in with an email address before checkout begins" data-modal-gallery="checkout-identity" loading="lazy">
              </div>
              <figcaption><strong>One entry point.</strong> Sign up or log in before starting checkout.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
                <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_solution01_1.png" alt="Enter the six-digit email code to verify your identity before checkout" data-modal-gallery="checkout-identity" loading="lazy">
              </div>
              <figcaption><strong>A one-time email code.</strong> No required password creation at the end of the order.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="cs-solution-head">
            <div class="cs-label">DURING CHECKOUT</div>
            <h3><span class="cs-solution-num">2 &middot;</span> Make checkout one page you can review</h3>
            <p class="checkout-ownership">My design.</p>
            <p class="cs-text">I combined five steps into one page. Each section is checked as you finish it, completed sections collapse, and errors show up next to the field that caused them. Renters see the whole order before placing it, with no surprise final step. On mobile, collapsed sections cut scrolling and the order button stays on screen once the order is ready to review.</p>
          </div>
          <figure>
            <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
              <img class="modal-target" src="screen_solution_01.gif" alt="Checkout prototype showing section validation and completed sections collapsing into a reviewable summary" data-modal-gallery="checkout-review" loading="lazy">
            </div>
            <figcaption class="cs-media-caption">Checkout prototype &middot; Complete a section, check it, and keep the order in view.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="checkout-annotations u-mt-md">
            <div><span class="cs-solution-num">01</span><p class="cs-text"><strong>Section checks.</strong> Errors appear beside the field that needs attention.</p></div>
            <div><span class="cs-solution-num">02</span><p class="cs-text"><strong>Collapsed summaries.</strong> Finished sections stay available for review.</p></div>
            <div><span class="cs-solution-num">03</span><p class="cs-text"><strong>A visible order button.</strong> On mobile, the final action stays on screen in review.</p></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="u-mt-md">
            <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
              <img class="modal-target" src="screen_solution_04_2.png" alt="Mobile checkout prototype showing the order button staying on screen in the review state and a reduced footer" data-modal-gallery="checkout-review" loading="lazy">
            </div>
            <figcaption class="cs-media-caption">Mobile prototype &middot; Less scrolling, with the final action in view.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="cs-solution-head">
            <div class="cs-label">DURING CHECKOUT</div>
            <h3><span class="cs-solution-num">3 &middot;</span> Close the exits back to deciding</h3>
            <p class="checkout-ownership">My call.</p>
            <p class="cs-text">I made the order-summary edit link that led back to the cart much less prominent, made trip editing secondary, and removed the &ldquo;how it works&rdquo; link and extra banners from checkout. Renters can still change their order, but the page no longer invites them to leave it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-before-after checkout-summary-comparison">
            <figure>
              <button class="checkout-summary-crop checkout-summary-crop--before modal-target" type="button" aria-label="Enlarge the legacy order summary" data-modal-gallery="checkout-summary">
                <img src="screenshot_legacy_process02.png" alt="Before: the legacy order summary includes a prominent Edit link back to the cart" loading="lazy">
              </button>
              <figcaption><strong>Before.</strong> The order summary invites another trip back to the cart.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <button class="checkout-summary-crop checkout-summary-crop--after modal-target" type="button" aria-label="Enlarge the redesigned order summary" data-modal-gallery="checkout-summary">
                <img src="mockup_desktop2.png" alt="After, design: a quieter collapsed order summary keeps attention on completing the rental" loading="lazy">
              </button>
              <figcaption><strong>After &middot; Design.</strong> A quieter order summary keeps the focus on completion.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <div class="cs-solution-head">
            <div class="cs-label">AFTER CHECKOUT</div>
            <h3><span class="cs-solution-num">4 &middot;</span> Move the survey to the confirmation page</h3>
            <p class="checkout-ownership">My proposal, finalized with the marketing VP.</p>
            <p class="cs-text">I had flagged the survey as friction earlier, and it was removed from the app. Marketing depended on those answers, so on web I proposed moving it rather than cutting it: after purchase, on the confirmation page, where it no longer stands between a renter and their booking. I worked with the marketing VP to narrow the questions to what they actually used.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cs-before-after">
            <figure>
              <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
                <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_solution03.png" alt="The marketing survey appears after purchase on the confirmation page" data-modal-gallery="checkout-confirmation" loading="lazy">
              </div>
              <figcaption><strong>The survey moves after purchase.</strong> Marketing can still collect the answers it uses.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <div class="cs-media checkout-screen">
                <img class="modal-target" src="screenshot_solution03_1.png" alt="Order confirmation with the survey link and app-download prompt after the booking is complete" data-modal-gallery="checkout-confirmation" loading="lazy">
              </div>
              <figcaption><strong>The booking comes first.</strong> The survey and app prompt follow confirmation.</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="results" class="cs-section cs-section--end container">
      <span id="impact" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <div class="cs-kicker">04 &middot; RESULTS</div>
      <h2 class="cs-h2">Checkout got smoother, and the remaining friction became visible</h2>

      <h3 class="cs-h3 u-mt-lg">The gain</h3>
      <table class="checkout-table">
        <caption>Start checkout &rarr; purchase</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Platform</th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">After</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Desktop web</th><td>88.6%</td><td class="checkout-result">91.2%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Mobile web</th><td>76.3%</td><td class="checkout-result">79.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Native app <span>(unchanged)</span></th><td>85.1%</td><td>84.2%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cs-stat-note">GA4 &middot; 4 weeks before vs. 4 weeks after launch.</p>
      <p class="cs-lead">Checkout completion rose on both web platforms while the unchanged app stayed flat over the same summer weeks. That makes seasonality a weaker explanation, though this isn't an A/B test.</p>

      <div class="checkout-trade u-mt-lg">
        <h3 class="cs-h3">The trade</h3>
        <p class="cs-text">Identity now comes before checkout, so some renters who used to drop out inside checkout now drop out just before it. Part of the gain reflects who enters checkout, not only a smoother form, and overall mobile-web purchases held about steady. The friction moved earlier, where leaving costs a renter seconds instead of a filled-out form.</p>
      </div>

      <h3 class="cs-h3 u-mt-lg">What's next</h3>
      <p class="cs-lead">The new login tracking showed exactly where the remaining friction is:</p>
      <table class="checkout-table u-mt-md">
        <caption>Of renters who start verification&hellip;</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Outcome</th><th scope="col">Desktop web</th><th scope="col">Mobile web</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Finish it</th><td>90%</td><td>75%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="cs-lead">On desktop, the trade paid off. On mobile web, about one in four people who start verification don't finish, likely because getting the code means leaving the browser. The next step is faster mobile verification: one-time-code autofill, magic links, or more prominent Google and Apple sign-in.</p>
      <%# Add the web survey response rate when it is available. %>

      <div class="cs-pullquote">
        <p>The redesign didn't make checkout shorter so much as clearer about what it's for. Once checkout held only order-critical tasks, what was left in the way became measurable, and it pointed straight at the next problem: <span class="accent">verification on mobile.</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <%- include(`${templatePath}/shared2026/footer`, {
    footerVariant: 'case-study',
    footerAllCaseStudiesHref: '/worklog',
    footerResumeHref: '/resume2026'
  }) %>

  <div class="wl-image-modal" id="wl-image-modal" aria-hidden="true">
    <button class="wl-image-modal__close" type="button" aria-label="Close image">&times;</button>
    <button class="wl-image-modal__control wl-image-modal__control--prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous image">&larr;</button>
    <figure class="wl-image-modal__figure">
      <img class="wl-image-modal__img" src="" alt="">
      <figcaption class="wl-image-modal__caption"></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <button class="wl-image-modal__control wl-image-modal__control--next" type="button" aria-label="Next image">&rarr;</button>
  </div>

</body>
</html>
